## Concrete Recommendations

### Fix #1 — Goroutine Lifecycle: Cancel TUN Reader on Session Teardown (Critical, Prevents 886s Stalls)

The 886-second `tun_read` max is the most urgent issue. TUN reader goroutines must have their blocking `Read()` interrupted when a session ends. The standard Go pattern using `context.Context` with a `cancel` function tied to session lifetime:

```go
// session.go — attach a context to every session's goroutines

type Session struct {
    id     string
    ctx    context.Context
    cancel context.CancelFunc
    tun    *water.Interface  // or your TUN implementation
    conn   net.Conn
    // ... other fields
}

func NewSession(parentCtx context.Context, tun *water.Interface, conn net.Conn) *Session {
    ctx, cancel := context.WithCancel(parentCtx)
    return &Session{
        ctx:    ctx,
        cancel: cancel,
        tun:    tun,
        conn:   conn,
    }
}

// TUN reader goroutine — must respect context cancellation.
// Problem: tun.Read() is a blocking syscall; context alone won't interrupt it.
// Solution: set a read deadline, then re-check context.
func (s *Session) tunReadLoop(out chan<- []byte) {
    buf := make([]byte, 65535)
    for {
        // Check cancellation before each read
        select {
        case <-s.ctx.Done():
            return  // ← THIS IS THE MISSING EXIT; goroutine currently never returns
        default:
        }

        // Set a short deadline so we wake up to check ctx.Done()
        // 200ms is short enough for prompt cleanup, long enough not to spin
        s.tun.SetReadDeadline(time.Now().Add(200 * time.Millisecond))

        n, err := s.tun.Read(buf)
        if err != nil {
            if netErr, ok := err.(net.Error); ok && netErr.Timeout() {
                // Deadline hit — loop back, check ctx.Done() above
                continue
            }
            // Real error — log and exit
            if s.ctx.Err() == nil {
                log.Printf("session %s: tun read error: %v", s.id, err)
            }
            return
        }

        packet := make([]byte, n)
        copy(packet, buf[:n])

        select {
        case out <- packet:
        case <-s.ctx.Done():
            return
        }
    }
}

// Session teardown — call this from your cleanup path
func (s *Session) Close() {
    s.cancel()          // signals all goroutines to exit
    s.conn.Close()      // unblocks any network reads
    // tun.Read() will unblock within 200ms due to deadline loop above
}
```

**Why not just `conn.Close()`?** Closing the network conn unblocks `obfs_read`, but the TUN reader is independent — it blocks on the OS TUN fd, not the network conn. You need the deadline+ctx pattern specifically for TUN.

**Expected impact:** Eliminates `tun_read` max outliers exceeding session duration. Reduces `full_egress` max from 886s to bounded value. Ensures prompt resource reclamation on disconnect.

